  "summary": "Tomorrow as imagined yesterday: fins, bubbles, chrome and unearned optimism.",
  "description": "Retrofuturism is the retrospective enjoyment of past futures: ray-gun rocketships, household robots, monorails, dome cities and atomic-age optimism as seen from after their promised dates failed to arrive. The term covers a family of styles — Atompunk's tailfins, Dieselpunk's riveted machinery, Raygun Gothic's swooping pulp craft — and a criticism tradition (paleofuturism) that studies why futures were imagined as they were. Designers mine it for warmth without nostalgia irony; manga and games borrow its confident chrome. The core appeal: a future that believed in itself, now viewable as period charm.",
  "culturalContext": "Retrofuturism is modern design's nostalgia engine: from Fallout's atompunk worlds to every \"yesterday's tomorrow\" brand campaign, the look packages past optimism as present charm; paleofuturist scholarship (blogs, books, archives) simultaneously studies why futures were imagined as they were — making retrofuture both aesthetic and intellectual discipline.",
